                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "\nDuration: 8 hours\n\n3 hours for tutorial campaign and 5 hours for main campaign.\n\nLegions of Steel is a computerised version of the 1992 board game.  It is very similar to Space Hulk and pits marines against machines (instead of genestealers).  \n\nThere are some nice options such as suppressing fire and advanced \"overwatch\" options. But it does feel like a lesser version of Space Hulk lacking in-depth customisation, alternate squads and close combat. \n\nThe tutorial teaches you how to play with some almost puzzle style scenarios (where shots always hit) to learn the rules.  Although I never did used advanced overwatch targeting in the main campaign.  \n\nThe main campaign had some fun scenarios, and most can be played in less than 15 minutes.  The campaign and missions did feel to lack depth.  There is multiplayer options which I did not try, but I don't believe there is much action to be harnessed.  \n\nOverall, I'd have to recommend Space Hulk:Ascension over Legions of Steel.",
